This repository is a SWI-Prolog application (portage-ng).

## Description 

The application reads Gentoo ebuild md5-cache files from a Gentoo portage tree repository into SWI-Prolog cache predicates. 
See cache.pl for the datastructure. The actual data is contained in Knowledge/kb.raw (which is qcompiled into Knowledge/kb.qlf).

- `Source/Knowledge/repository.pl`: enables controlling of Portage tree (syncing, loading, saving facts)
- `Source/Knowledge/knowledgebase.pl`: different repositories register themselves with the knowledge base. 
- `Source/Knowledge/cache.pl`: the prolog cache data structure 
- `Source/Domain/Gentoo/eapi.pl`: a Prolog DCG grammar to read md5-cache syntax
- `Source/Domain/Gentoo/Rules/resolving.pl`: domain specific (Gentoo) specific rules handling dependencies, interpreting use flags, PDEPEND hooks, etc.
- `Source/Domain/Gentoo/vdb.pl`: a repository containing information about installed ebuilds
- `Source/Domain/Gentoo/pkgmoves.pl`: profiles/updates package move / slotmove handling (read-time translation of targets, world/set entries and computed-set tree lookups; world file rewritten post-sync via `pkgmoves:apply_world_moves`; VDB rewriting intentionally left to traditional Portage)
- `Source/Domain/Gentoo/Exceptions/fixup.pl`: generic registry/dispatcher for build-time domain exception fixups (multifile hooks `fixup:mechanism/1`, `fixup:phase_retry_hook/10`, `fixup:mechanism_note/3`; record via `fixup:record/3`; shared same-version repair rebuild helper `fixup:repair_rebuild/4`); `collision.pl` (#90 collision deconfliction), `ghcabi.pl` (#93 GHC ABI repair, native haskell-updater; #108 boot-lib recache; #112 prove-time `CABAL_CORE_LIB_GHC_PV` filter) and `ocamlabi.pl` (#99 OCaml/findlib ABI repair) register here
- `Source/Application/Performance/sampler.pl`: performance sampling and instrumentation (hook perf, test_stats counters, ctx_union sampling)
- `Source/Application/System/subprocess.pl`: generic external process spawning and dns-sd interface
- `Source/Application/interface.pl`: CLI option parsing, TTY init, request dispatching
- `Source/Application/Mode/cluster.pl`: high-level orchestration for distributed proving across workers
- `Source/Application/Mode/worker.pl`: compute node for distributed proving (polls server for jobs)
- `Source/Application/Mode/server.pl`: Pengine HTTP server + job/result queues for distributed proving
- `Source/Application/Llm/semantic.pl`: semantic (vector) search over the knowledge base via Ollama embeddings
- `Source/Pipeline/Builder/buildtime.pl`: build time estimation from VDB sizes and emerge.log history


## Architecture (mental model)
Pipeline (standalone): reader/parser → resolver → orderer → printer → builder.

The prover is a generic inference facility, not a stage: it takes the rule
module to prove against as its first argument (`prover:prove(Rules, ...)`,
`prover:prove_once(Rules, ...)`) and knows nothing about resolving or
ordering. The two proving stages are thin wrappers that pair the prover
with a rule set:

- reader/parser build cache facts using eapi grammar
- resolver (pass 1, "what"): `resolver:resolve/9` =
  `prover:prove(resolving, ...)` — proofs (ProofAVL, ModelAVL,
  Constraints, TriggersAVL) over the Gentoo domain rules
- orderer (pass 2, "when"): `orderer:order/5` =
  `prover:prove_once(ordering, ...)` + wave projection:
  every pass-1 step must prove `scheduled/1`; availability proofs assign
  waves; cycles fall through a `currently_proving` guard into a VDB "world"
  citation or a negative `unreachable/2` domain assumption; soft preferences
  (RDEPEND groups, `order_after` anchors, PDEPEND completion, assumed-dep
  aliases) are honored by the projection exactly when they close no cycle
- printer renders plan + assumptions & warnings

Callers outside any pass (query-side model construction) fall back to
`config:default_rules/1` (= `resolving`).

The classic wave planner (Kahn) + SCC scheduler (Kosaraju, `:run`
relaxation, merge-sets, repair passes) were retired after tree-wide A/B
comparison showed identical action multisets and identical runtime.
See `Documentation/Designs/ordering-engine.md`.

Key files:
- `Source/Pipeline/prover.pl`: generic proof engine, parameterized by rule module; cycles become prover cycle-break assumptions (`assumed(rule(Lit))` in proof, `assumed(Lit)` in model).
- `Source/Pipeline/resolver.pl`: resolve stage — `resolver:resolve/9` plus the resolve-stage test harnesses (`resolver:test/1`, `resolver:test_latest/1`, `resolver:test_stats/1`, `resolver:test_stats_pkgs/2`).
- `Source/Domain/Gentoo/Rules/resolving.pl` (+ `Rules/Resolving/` submodules): resolve rule set; domain assumptions appear as `rule(assumed(X), [])` and show as “Domain assumptions”.
- `Source/Pipeline/orderer.pl`: order stage — pass-2 driver `orderer:order/5`, wave projection, preference honoring, merge-order bias (within-wave refcount ordering), `orderer:test/1` harnesses.
- `Source/Domain/Gentoo/Rules/ordering.pl`: order rule set — planning laws (`rule/2`: scheduled/available/unreachable) + Gentoo bindings `step/1`, `requires/2`, `prefers/2` (runtime groups, order_after, PDEPEND completion #18/#19, configure closure #21, assumed-dep alias #95), `world/2` (VDB citations).
- `Source/Pipeline/pipeline.pl`: orchestrates resolver+orderer. Two canonical entry points with 5-tier committed-choice progressive relaxation (strict, keyword_acceptance, blockers, unmask, keyword_unmask): `prove_plan_with_fallback/5` (full pipeline) and `prove_with_fallback/4` (resolve only, for layered tests and --bugs).
- `Source/Pipeline/printer.pl`: output + warnings; distinguishes domain assumptions vs prover cycle-breaks.
- `Source/Domain/Gentoo/version.pl`: version domain model (`version_domain(Slots, Bounds)`), domain meet/intersection, consistency checks.

Version representation:
- Versions are stored as `version/7` compound terms: `version(NumsNorm, Alpha, SuffixRank, SuffixNum, SuffixTail, Rev, Full)`. SuffixTail holds the remaining suffixes as `s(Rank, Num)` pairs closed by the `s(4,0)` "(final)" terminator (`[]` when the version has no suffix), so multi-suffix versions compare pairwise per PMS.
- Empty/absent versions use the atom `version_none`.
- Comparison uses standard `compare/3` directly on the compound term (no runtime key conversion).

PDEPEND handling:
- PDEPEND is always enabled; there is no preference flag for it.
- PDEPEND deps are resolved single-pass inside the prover via the
  `heuristic:proof_obligation/4` hook.

Assumption taxonomy (do not mix these up):

- Domain assumptions (rules-level): proof key `rule(assumed(X))`
- Prover cycle-break assumptions: proof key `assumed(rule(X))`

### Domain assumption polarity (how to talk about them)

"Plan has domain assumptions" is NOT inherently bad. When reporting or
summarising a run (in chat or in commit/PR text), always split domain
assumptions by polarity and say which kind you mean — never lump them into a
single scary count:

- POSITIVE / actionable — the plan IS satisfiable once the user makes a
  config change; the assumption itself is the suggested action:
    - `masked`             → add to package.unmask / adjust package.mask
    - `keyword_filtered`   → accept ~arch (package.accept_keywords)
    - license acceptance   → accept license (package.license)
    - `blocker_assumption` → resolve a blocker (unmerge / pick alternative)
  Phrase these as "here is what to change", not "this is broken".

- NEGATIVE / blocking — no simple config action resolves it; the request is
  structurally unsatisfiable as stated:
    - `non_existent_dependency`                     → package/version not in tree
    - `missing_dependency`                          → dependency not found
    - `required_use_violation`                      → impossible USE requirement
    - `slot_conflict`                               → conflicting slots
    - `version_no_candidate` / `version_conflict`   → no version satisfies
    - `unsatisfied_constraints`                     → constraints cannot be met
    - `issue_with_model`                            → no consistent model found
  These are the ones that indicate a genuine problem.

Classification lives in `Source/Pipeline/Printer/Plan/assumption.pl`
(`assumption:assumption_type/2`, `assumption:assumption_reason_type/2`).
Prover cycle-breaks (`*_cycle`, `naf_cycle`) are a separate, benign axis
(exit 1), not domain assumptions.

## "Context" disambiguation (do not conflate these)

The word "context" has three distinct meanings in this codebase:

1. **OO context** (`Source/Logic/context.pl`): The Logtalk-style meta-object
   protocol providing class declaration, instance creation, and visibility
   guards (`dpublic`, `dprotected`, `dprivate`).  Used by `repository.pl`,
   `knowledgebase.pl`, `stat.pl`, etc.  Recognisable by `:this`, `::`, `::=`,
   `::-`.  In comments, refer to this as "OO context" or "instance context".

2. **Proof-term context** (`?{Context}`): A plain Prolog list wrapped in `{}`
   and attached to every proof literal: `Repo://Entry:Action?{[...]}`.
   Carries parameters like `required_use/1`, `build_with_use/1`, `self/1`,
   `constraint/1`, slot info, etc. — a lightweight Zeller-style feature term
   threaded through proof search.  In comments, refer to this as "proof
   context", "literal context", or "`?{Context}` list".

3. **Pengines / sandbox context**: The execution environment used in
   client-server mode (`Source/Application/Mode/server.pl`).  Pengines create
   a sandboxed "context" with thread-local clauses.  In comments, refer to
   this as "Pengines context" or "sandbox context".

When writing comments, always qualify which meaning is intended.  Avoid bare
"context" or "in context" when it could refer to more than one of the above.


## Learned constraint refinement

The prover offers a generic learned constraint store that persists across
proof retries (managed by `with_cn_domain_reprove_state`):

- `prover:learn(Literal, Constraint, Added)` — store/merge a constraint
- `prover:learned(Literal, Constraint)` — look up a learned constraint
- Merge semantics: `feature_unification:val_hook` (e.g., domain intersection)
- Literal format: `cn_domain(Category, Name, Slot)` for version domains

The resolving rule set uses this store in four ways:
1. **Candidate narrowing**: `grouped_dep_effective_domain` intersects the
   local+context domain with any learned domain (`apply_learned_domain`).
2. **Conflict learning**: constraint guards learn the domain when a conflict
   is detected (alongside the existing reject-based reprove mechanism).
3. **Parent narrowing**: `maybe_learn_parent_narrowing` learns to exclude the
   parent version when a child dep can't be satisfied (wrong-level fix).
4. **Wildcard domain learning**: `maybe_learn_wildcard_domain` derives an
   upper-bound cn_domain from a wildcard dep (e.g. `=pkg-0.6*` → `< 0.7`)
   when parent narrowing alone failed or is futile (single-version parent).

Self-dep handling:
- `is_self_dep/5` detects bootstrap self-deps (same C/N/slot as parent).
- Cross-slot self-deps (same C/N, different slot) are regular dependencies.

Dep priority ordering (`dep_priority/2`):
- BaseK=1 (tight upper bound), 4 (tilde), 8 (wildcard), 999 (unconstrained).
- Tighter constraints proved first to lock `selected_cn` early.

Inspired by Zeller's feature logic (incremental domain narrowing) and
Vermeir/Van Nieuwenborgh's ordered logic programs (priority-based conflict
resolution). See `Documentation/Handbook/21-doc-resolver-comparison.md`.

### Testing learned constraints
When testing changes to the learned constraint mechanism, ALWAYS verify:
1. Exit code (0 = no assumptions, 1 = cycle breaks only, 2 = domain assumptions)
2. "Total: N actions" line present (proof completed, not crashed)
3. Count of "non-existent" lines
4. No "Unknown message" or escaping exceptions in output
5. Runtime (should be < 10 seconds for single targets)


## Naming conventions

- Source filenames must NOT contain hyphens (`-`) or underscores (`_`).
  Use concatenated lowercase words (e.g. `gentoo.pl`, `knowledgebase.pl`).
 Exceptions (grandfathered, do not add new ones):
 - `portage-ng.pl` (project entry point / name)
 - `binpkg_exec.pl`, `binpkg_index.pl`, `binpkg_extract.pl`,
 `ebuild_exec.pl`, `missing_provider.pl` (underscore-named for
 readability of their prefixes; their module names match the filename)
 - host-local templates under `Source/Config/Private/` (configuration,
 not source modules)
- Prolog module names follow the same rule (e.g. `:- module(gentoo, [])`).
- Subdirectory names under `Source/` may use CamelCase (e.g. `Application/`, `Domain/`, `Config/`, `Pipeline/`).


## Source file documentation style

Every `.pl` source file follows a strict layout. Use `Source/Application/System/bonjour.pl` as the
canonical reference.

### 1. File header (block comment)

```prolog
/*
  Author:   Pieter Van den Abeele
  E-mail:   pvdabeel@mac.com
  Copyright (c) 2005-2026, Pieter Van den Abeele

  Distributed under the terms of the LICENSE file in the root directory of this
  project.
*/
```

- One blank line after the closing `*/`.

### 2. Module documentation (PlDoc)

```prolog
/** <module> MODULE_NAME_UPPERCASE
Short one-line description.

Optional longer description (may span multiple lines).
*/
```

- Module name in the `<module>` tag is UPPERCASE (e.g. `BONJOUR`, `INDEX`).
- One blank line after the closing `*/`.

### 3. Module declaration

```prolog
:- module(modulename, []).
```

- One blank line after the module declaration.

### 4. Chapter header (one per file, immediately after module declaration)

```prolog
% =============================================================================
%  MODULE_NAME_UPPERCASE declarations
% =============================================================================
```

- Exactly one `=====` chapter per file, right after `:- module`.
- Title format: `MODULE_NAME_UPPERCASE declarations`.
- One blank line after the closing `%  ===...` line.

### 5. Section headers (all subsequent sections)

```prolog
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
%  Section title
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
```

- All sections after the chapter header use `-----` dashes.
- Title is a short descriptive phrase (e.g. `Advertise a service`,
  `Dependency annotation helpers`).
- One blank line after the closing `%  ---...` line.

### 6. Predicate documentation (PlDoc)

```prolog
%! module:predicate_name(+Arg1, -Arg2)
%
% Short description of what the predicate does.

module:predicate_name(Arg1, Arg2) :-
  body.
```

- `%!` line declares the predicate with mode indicators (`+`, `-`, `?`).
- `%` blank doc-comment line separates the signature from the description.
- Description is one or more `%` lines.
- One blank line between the description and the clause head.
- Two blank lines between consecutive predicates.
- Multi-clause predicates: no blank line between clauses of the same predicate;
  two blank lines before the next predicate.

### 7. Spacing summary

| Element                        | Blank lines after |
|-------------------------------|-------------------|
| File header `*/`               | 1                 |
| PlDoc module comment `*/`      | 1                 |
| `:- module(...)` declaration   | 1                 |
| `=====` chapter header         | 1                 |
| `-----` section header         | 1                 |
| Predicate doc + last clause    | 2                 |
| Between clauses of same pred   | 0                 |
| End of file (after last clause)| 0 (no trailing blank line) |

## Configuration

### Portage tree locations
- `Source/Config/<host>.local.pl` (e.g. `Source/Config/mac-pro.local.pl`): contains the host-specific configuration (location of repository, md5-cache, vdb pkg installation data); `Source/Config/default.pl` holds the defaults

### /etc/portage configuration
- `Source/Domain/Gentoo/Preference/userconfig.pl`: reads standard Gentoo /etc/portage user configuration files (make.conf, package.use, package.mask, etc.)
- `Source/Domain/Gentoo/Preference/UserConfig/`: template files mirroring /etc/portage layout
- `config:portage_confdir/1`: path to the configuration directory

### Profile loading
- `Source/Domain/Gentoo/profile.pl`: profile reading + cache serialization/deserialization (Knowledge/profile.qlf)
- `config:profile_loading(Mode, Strategy)`: per-mode strategy (`live` or `cached`)
- `--sync` generates `Knowledge/profile.qlf` via `profile:cache_save`
- When `cached` is set but `Knowledge/profile.qlf` is missing, falls back to `live`

### Graph location
- `Source/Config/<host>.local.pl`: config:graph_directory/1 holds the location of the graph directory that contains .emerge files (traditional gentoo emerge output for a given package). 

### Configuration 
- `Source/config.pl`: configuration file with various configuration settings



## How to run (IMPORTANT)
- Do NOT run ad-hoc `swipl -g "..."` snippets; they often miss required operator defs, libraries, and module load order.
- Always launch via the project wrapper (project root):
  - `./Source/Application/Wrapper/portage-ng-dev --mode standalone --pretend <target>`
  - `./Source/Application/Wrapper/portage-ng-dev --mode standalone --shell`
  - Non-interactive / CI-style: `./Source/Application/Wrapper/portage-ng-dev --mode standalone --ci --pretend <target>`
    Exit codes (current — see `interface:ci_exit_code/3`):
      - 0 = no assumptions
      - 1 = only prover cycle-break assumptions
      - 2 = any domain assumptions (e.g. missing/non-existent deps)

    Exit codes (PLANNED extension — NOT yet implemented). Goal: let
    tinderbox-ng infer the *reason* from the exit code alone, distinguishing
    actionable (positive) from blocking (negative) outcomes, and separating
    resolver outcomes from build failures. See "Domain assumption polarity"
    above for the positive/negative split.
      Resolver / plan (`--pretend`) outcomes:
        - 0 = clean plan, no assumptions
        - 1 = prover cycle-break assumptions only (benign)
        - 2 = POSITIVE / actionable domain assumptions only
              (unmask / keyword / license / blocker)
        - 3 = NEGATIVE / blocking domain assumptions
              (non-existent, unsatisfiable USE, no version, no model)
      Build (`--merge`) failures — distinct higher range so a failed *build*
      is never confused with a resolver outcome:
        - 10 = restricted / manual download required (RESTRICT=fetch)
        - 11 = download / network failure
        - 12 = configuration / dependency issue at build time
        - 13 = upstream compile failure
        - 14 = file collision
        - 15 = test-phase failure
        - 16 = sandbox / permission violation
      Numbers above are a PROPOSAL — confirm before implementing. Avoid
      124 (wrapper hard-timeout) and 126/127/128+ (shell/signal). When
      implemented, update `interface:ci_exit_code/3`, the builder failure
      paths (`Source/Pipeline/builder.pl`, `ebuild_exec.pl`, `download.pl`),
      this list, and `tinderbox-ng analyze` together.

- For resolver/debug target runs in this workflow, always include `--pretend` to avoid
  mutating local state (for example `Source/Knowledge/Sets/world/*.local`).

### Preferred scripted workflow (here-doc “piping”)
For quick iteration and for reproducible debugging, prefer running a *scripted* Prolog session via `--shell` and a here-doc:

```sh
./Source/Application/Wrapper/portage-ng-dev --mode standalone --shell --timeout 60 <<'PL'
resolver:test_stats(portage).
% Any other queries you want:
% resolver:test_stats_pkgs(portage, ['kde-apps'-'kde-apps-meta']).
halt.
PL
```

This keeps the full project load graph intact (same as interactive `--shell`), while letting you run multi-line “scripts” non-interactively.

### Running test_stats in Cursor (IMPORTANT)
The full `resolver:test_stats(portage)` run processes ~31k ebuilds and takes
~2.5 minutes. Its progress lines overflow Cursor’s 1MB terminal buffer,
so the stats summary at the end gets truncated. **Always redirect to a file**:

```sh
./Source/Application/Wrapper/portage-ng-dev --mode standalone --shell --timeout 600 <<'PL' > /tmp/portage-ng-test-stats.log 2>&1
resolver:test_stats(portage).
halt.
PL
```

Then read `/tmp/portage-ng-test-stats.log` to inspect the results.
Use `block_until_ms: 0` to background the command and poll with `Await`.

For spot-checking a few packages (fast, no file redirect needed):

```sh
./Source/Application/Wrapper/portage-ng-dev --mode standalone --shell --timeout 60 <<'PL'
resolver:test_stats_pkgs(portage, ['dev-libs'-'glib', 'app-misc'-'geneweb']).
halt.
PL
```

### test_stats vs .merge accuracy
`resolver:test_stats` uses `prove_with_fallback` (resolve only) while `.merge`
files use `prove_plan_with_fallback` (full pipeline). Both share the same
5-tier fallback chain and produce **identical domain assumptions per ebuild**.
Aggregate differences between test_stats and the compare report are explained by:
- Coverage: test_stats runs all ebuilds; compare report only includes pairs
  with both `.merge` and `.emerge` files
- Versions: `test_stats_pkgs` tests latest versions only; `.merge` covers all
- Cycle breaks: `config:print_prover_cycles(false)` suppresses the cycle
  break section in `.merge` output, so the compare script reports 0
- Blockers: count differences track the coverage gap above

Bottom line: `resolver:test_stats` is a valid substitute for regenerating
`.merge` files when checking domain assumption accuracy.

The canonical load graph for standalone is in `portage-ng.pl`:
- `load_common_modules/0`
- `load_standalone_modules/0`

### Non-interactive / sandbox robustness
- Terminal size lookups must go through `config:printing_tty_size/2` (direct `tty_size/2` may throw in non-tty environments).
- Do NOT run `--sync` / repository syncing from inside the sandbox. The user will run `--sync` externally when needed.
- Do NOT create `metadata/md5-cache/*` manually. `--sync` (egencache/md5-cache generation) produces those automatically.
- Do NOT run `--graph` / repository graphing from inside the sandbox. The user will run `--graph` externally when needed.

## Performance/engineering notes
- Proof/Model/Triggers are assoc/AVL-based; avoid repeated full traversals when possible.
- Query performance uses `goal_expansion/2` macros in `Source/Knowledge/query.pl`.
- `query:search(model(dependency(Merged,Phase)):config?{Ctx}, Repo://Id)` is
  cached per proof (`memo:dep_model_cache_/5`, gated by
  `config:dep_model_cache/1`).  The goal-expansion inlines self-context
  injection, `prove_model`, and `group_dependencies` at compile time.
  Model construction depends on mutable proof state, so every hazard is
  encoded in the cache key rather than clearing the cache (two earlier
  clear-based attempts were unsound):
  (1) `build_with_use` context varies per dependency path,
  (2) `prover:assuming` flags change between fallback attempts,
  (3) `memo:selected_cn_snap_` evolves during the proof (affects OR group ordering),
  (4) `variant:use_override` / `variant:branch_prefer` change during variant mode.
  Profiling showed 25-30% of proving time in these queries, with up to 88%
  of calls redundant for complex packages. See top-of-file comment in `query.pl`.

## Development flow
- We use a git workflow.
- Start development always with clean committed state.
- When development is done, ask the user to run a tinderbox-ng regression
  test on `vm-linux.local` (see "Resolver regression testing via
  tinderbox-ng" below). The resulting `analysis.json` / `analysis.txt`
  diff is the authoritative regression check; it replaces the legacy
  `--graph` + plan-comparison loop, which has been removed from this
  repo and migrated to the
  [tinderbox-ng](https://github.com/pvdabeel/tinderbox-ng) repository.
- Ask user for permission to commit.


## Resolver regression testing via tinderbox-ng (vm-linux.local)

After a resolver change (resolving rules, prover, resolver, orderer, printer)
the authoritative regression check is a side-by-side compare against
traditional Gentoo `emerge` run by
[tinderbox-ng](https://github.com/pvdabeel/tinderbox-ng) on the Linux
test VM `vm-linux.local`. It runs the same target through both engines
in fresh, identical OverlayFS sessions and diffs their plan/build logs.

### After-commit workflow

```sh
# 1. From this macOS host, push the new portage-ng source into the VM
#    baseline. Does NOT regenerate kb.qlf — only do that (refresh-kb)
#    after parser / grammar / knowledge-base schema changes; resolver
#    edits (prover / resolver / orderer / printer / rule sets) need only
#    refresh-portage-ng.
cd ~/Desktop/tinderbox-ng
bin/deploy-host.sh --refresh-portage-ng root@vm-linux.local

# 2. Quick per-target sanity check (plan only, fresh sessions on
#    both sides, both destroyed at the end):
ssh root@vm-linux.local 'tinderbox-ng compare <category>/<package>'

# 3. Whole-tree matrix regression inside a fresh session:
ssh root@vm-linux.local 'tinderbox-ng new regress'
ssh root@vm-linux.local 'tinderbox-ng exec regress -- \
  tinderbox-matrix resolver \
  /usr/local/share/tinderbox-ng/share/tinderbox-ng/manifest-1000.txt'

# 4. Analyse the latest matrix run (auto-detects the most recent
#    /srv/tinderbox-ng/reports/compare-matrix-<stamp>/).
#    --md5-cache enables dep-aware metrics (DepConc%, Viol%):
ssh root@vm-linux.local 'tinderbox-ng analyze \
  --md5-cache /srv/tinderbox-ng/baseline/var/db/repos/gentoo/metadata/md5-cache'

# 5. Pull the structured analysis back for review:
scp root@vm-linux.local:'/srv/tinderbox-ng/reports/compare-matrix-*/analysis.{json,txt}' /tmp/
```

`tinderbox-ng analyze` writes `analysis.json` + `analysis.txt` into the
matrix run directory; both replace the old per-commit comparison
snapshots that previously lived in the now-removed `Reports/` directory.

### When to run what

- `tinderbox-ng compare <pkg>` — quick smoke after a one-off rule edit.
  Per-target only, both engines run in parallel, output lands in
  `/srv/tinderbox-ng/logs/compare-<label>-<stamp>/`.
- `tinderbox-matrix resolver <manifest>` inside a session — full
  regression. Use `manifest-100.txt` for smoke and `manifest-1000.txt`
  for serious checks; both ship under
  `/usr/local/share/tinderbox-ng/share/tinderbox-ng/` in the deployed
  layout.
- `tinderbox-ng refresh-kb` on the VM — ONLY after parser / grammar /
  knowledge-base changes (regenerates `kb.qlf`). For prover / resolver / orderer /
  printer / rules edits, `refresh-portage-ng` is enough.
- `tinderbox-ng phase-stats` — consolidate per-session
  `phase_stats.pl` files into a master forecast for build-time
  estimation; orthogonal to the merge-vs-emerge regression check.

## Compare merge vs emerge plans (metric semantics)

The merge-vs-emerge comparison is driven by `tinderbox-ng analyze` (see
"Resolver regression testing via tinderbox-ng" above). The metric
formulas below are canonical and shared with the tinderbox-ng repo's
`.cursorrules`, which intentionally does NOT duplicate them — defer to
this section as the source of truth.

- Inputs (per package, produced inside a tinderbox-ng session):
  - `portage-ng.plan.log`: portage-ng `--pretend` output (the same
    plan text that ends up in `*.merge` for the on-disk graph
    directory).
  - `emerge.plan.log`: traditional `emerge -vp` output.
  - The configuration on both sides is identical: same Portage tree
    pin, same VDB, same `/etc/portage`. `tinderbox-ng` enforces this
    via `_patch_portage_ng_host_config` and the immutable baseline.

- Analyzer output (per matrix run, in
  `/srv/tinderbox-ng/reports/compare-matrix-<stamp>/`):
  - `analysis.json` — full structured metrics dump (use `jq` to drill in).
  - `analysis.txt`  — captured stdout summary (line-oriented overview).

- Useful output fields inside `analysis.json`:
  - `aggregate_counts` (missing/extra/version/use/action totals)
  - `aggregate_assumptions` (domain assumptions / blockers / cycle breaks)
  - `top_missing_cpn`, `top_extra_cpn`, `top_domain_assumptions`

- When comparing two `analysis.json` files (baseline vs current
  branch), provide output in a table:
  - Columns: previous analysis vs current analysis
  - Rows: % vs portage merge_ok for CN, CN+V, CN+V+U, Order%,
    #blockers, #cycle breaks, #domain assumptions

- Canonical metric formula for `% vs portage merge_ok` rows (use this exact formula):
  - Filter to `emerge_ok == true` pairs only.
  - For each pair define:
    - `e = counts.emerge_pkgs`
    - `m = counts.missing_in_merge`
    - `x = counts.extra_in_merge`
    - `v = counts.version_mismatches`
    - `u = counts.use_mismatches` (not `use_mismatches_common`)
    - `i_cnv = e - m`
    - `u_cnv = e + x`
  - Aggregate by summing across all `emerge_ok` pairs:
    - `inter_cnv = Σ(i_cnv)`, `union_cnv = Σ(u_cnv)`
    - `inter_cn  = Σ(i_cnv + v)`, `union_cn  = Σ(u_cnv - v)`
    - `inter_cnvu = Σ(i_cnv - u)`, `union_cnvu = Σ(u_cnv + u)`
  - Final percentages:
    - `CN      = 100 * inter_cn / union_cn`
    - `CN+V    = 100 * inter_cnv / union_cnv`
    - `CN+V+U  = 100 * inter_cnvu / union_cnvu`
  - Ordering concordance:
    - `Order% = 100 * (Σ order_pairs - Σ order_inversions) / Σ order_pairs`
    - Uses `counts.order_inversions` and `counts.order_pairs` from `emerge_ok` pairs
    - Or from `order_aggregate.order_concordance_pct` in the report JSON
  - Count rows in the same table are also `emerge_ok`-only sums:
    - `#blockers = Σ assumptions.blockers`
    - `#cycle breaks = Σ assumptions.cycle_breaks`
    - `#domain assumptions = Σ assumptions.domain_assumptions`

- The table output is used to determine regressions.


## Compare tooling (authoritative paths + usage)

Regression tooling is hosted in two places:

- **Merge-vs-emerge plan comparison** lives in the
  [tinderbox-ng](https://github.com/pvdabeel/tinderbox-ng) repository
  at `share/tinderbox-ng/compare-merge-emerge.py`. It is invoked
  automatically by `tinderbox-ng analyze`, and exposed for one-off
  per-target runs via `tinderbox-ng compare`. Do NOT vendor this
  script back into portage-ng; the source of truth is the tinderbox-ng
  repo.
- **md5-cache extractor regression** lives in this repo:
  `md5cache_validate/0,1` in `Source/Test/unittest.pl`. It re-extracts
  metadata for every md5-cache entry through
  `Source/Domain/Gentoo/Ebuild/ebuild-depend.sh --batch` and diffs the
  result key by key against the on-disk cache.
- Do not create ad-hoc compare scripts outside these two locations.

### Validate ebuild-depend.sh against md5-cache
- Spot check (20 ebuilds, ~1 second):
  ```sh
  ./Source/Application/Wrapper/portage-ng-dev --mode standalone --shell <<'PL'
  load_files(portage('Source/Test/unittest'), [if(true)]).
  md5cache_validate([limit(20)]).
  halt.
  PL
  ```
- Full tree with persisted report:
  ```sh
  ./Source/Application/Wrapper/portage-ng-dev --mode standalone --shell <<'PL'
  load_files(portage('Source/Test/unittest'), [if(true)]).
  md5cache_validate([out('/tmp/md5cache_report.pl')]).
  halt.
  PL
  ```

## Running emerge -vp on macOS (comparison tool)

A gentoo-prefix environment allows running `emerge -vp` on macOS for quick
comparison against portage-ng output. The install location is per-host and
not hardcoded anywhere outside `Source/config.pl` and the user's shell rc.

### Discovering the prefix on the current host
- Authoritative path: `config:emerge_vp_path/1` in the per-host config file
  `Source/Config/<host>.local.pl`. The prefix root is the
  parent of the `bin/` directory in that path. Examples present today:
  - `imac-pro.local`    → `/Volumes/Disk 1/gentoo-prefix`
  - `mac-pro.local`     → `/Volumes/Storage/gentoo-prefix`
  - `macbook-pro.local` → `/opt/local/gentoo-prefix`
- Convenience: if `emerge-vp` is on `$PATH` (the user's `.zshrc` typically
  prepends `<prefix>/bin`), `command -v emerge-vp` resolves it; the prefix
  is then `dirname "$(dirname "$(command -v emerge-vp)")"`.
- Adding a new host: add a `config:emerge_vp_path(Path)` fact in
  `Source/Config/<host>.local.pl` and (optionally) add `<prefix>/bin` to the
  user's `PATH` in `~/.zshrc`. The same file is the place for
  `config:ebuild_command/1` if it needs to point at
  `<prefix>/bin/ebuild-prefix`.

### Setup
- Installed via the external `emerge-install.sh` script (run once per machine).
- Requires a local clone of the portage source (e.g. `~/Git/portage`).
- Config files under `<prefix>/etc/portage/` are symlinked from the
  portage-ng config directory (`Source/Config/Gentoo/`).

### Usage
The wrappers compute their own `EPREFIX` from `$0`, so the prefix can live
anywhere on disk. Set `PREFIX` to the value resolved above, then:

```sh
PREFIX=/opt/local/gentoo-prefix    # or whatever this host uses

# Quick pretend (uses PORTAGE_DIR env or auto-detects):
PORTAGE_DIR=~/Git/portage/bin "$PREFIX/bin/emerge-vp" sys-apps/portage

# General wrapper (pass any emerge flags):
PORTAGE_DIR=~/Git/portage/bin "$PREFIX/bin/emerge-wrapper" --info
```

If `<prefix>/bin` is on `$PATH`, the bare `emerge-vp` / `emerge-wrapper`
commands work directly with no `PREFIX` indirection.

### Key design decision
- The wrappers set `EPREFIX` (and patch `portage.const.EPREFIX`) to the prefix
  path. This tells portage it is running in a Gentoo Prefix environment, so it
  creates a single config tree. Profile, VDB, and cache all resolve under the
  prefix directory.
- VDB is consulted via `<prefix>/var/db/pkg` (symlinked to the portage-ng
  VDB), so installed packages correctly show as `[nomerge]`.
- Nothing inside the prefix tree itself uses absolute self-references, so the
  whole `<prefix>` directory is relocatable: move it, update
  `config:emerge_vp_path/1` (and `config:ebuild_command/1` if used) plus the
  user's `PATH`, and it keeps working.

## Compare to portage source code
- Tranditional emerge is available on this system and uses the same Portage 
  tree and VDB pkg as portage-ng. The alias emerge-vp can be called to compare
  with traditional emerge.
- The portage source code can be found using `type -p emerge`.